    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "But isn't that just a way of intellectualizing what is essentially a failure of skill? It seems like there's a very strong bias in the academic and institutional world where if a piece is \"conceptually rigorous\" or \"challenging,\" it's automatically deemed important, regardless of whether it actually holds up as art. Is it really a \"strategy\" to bypass beauty, or is it just that the gatekeepers of the art world have decided that political utility is more important than aesthetic excellence?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is fair to observe that institutional validation often confers importance, which can create a feedback loop where concepts become prioritized over execution. However, labeling it solely as a \"failure of skill\" might be too reductionist, as many artists deliberately choose modes of expression that eschew traditional virtuosity. Rather than a simple bypass, it represents a shift in the historical criteria of judgment, moving from a focus on Mimesis (imitation/beauty) to a focus on Critique (conceptual friction). Whether this marks a \"decline\" or a \"reorientation\" in artistic purpose remains one of contemporary art's most significant debates.",
      "source": "target",
